\description{
Quantifies how much misspecification of the population Pearson
correlation distorts an AIPE-based sample size plan. On each
replication the function draws an \emph{n}-row sample from a
bivariate normal distribution with correlation \code{true_cor} and
computes the sample correlation and its Fisher's \eqn{Z} CI, the
interval \code{\link{ss_aipe_r}} plans for and
\code{\link{correlations_test}} reports. Because the back-transformed
width is largest at \eqn{\rho = 0} and shrinks as \eqn{|\rho|} grows,
a planning value whose magnitude overstates the population
correlation yields realized intervals wider than planned, and the
summary rows report by how much.
}
\section{The record of the simulation}{

Every sensitivity function of the package summarizes its replications
over one denominator: the replications that produced a confidence
interval (for a power sensitivity function, a test). The proportion of
intervals that met the width target (\code{pct_ci_less_width}), the
proportions of intervals that missed the true value, and the summaries
of the widths are all taken over those replications. A replication
without an interval is in none of these rows. It is counted, and the
returned table carries the counts in its attribute \code{simulation},
a list read with \code{attr(x, "simulation")}:
\describe{
  \item{\code{attempted}}{the number of replications asked for
    (\code{G}).}
  \item{\code{returned}}{the number that produced an interval (a
    test).}
  \item{\code{failed}}{the number that did not
    (\code{attempted - returned}).}
  \item{\code{denominator}}{the number of replications the rows named
    above are taken over, which is \code{returned}.}
  \item{\code{width_target_probability}}{the proportion of the
    replications with an interval whose interval met the width target,
    the value of the \code{pct_ci_less_width} row; \code{NA} for a
    function or a call without a width target, and when no replication
    produced an interval.}
  \item{\code{width_target_mc_ci}}{the exact binomial
    (Clopper-Pearson) limits for that proportion, at the confidence
    level of the table. They describe the error of the simulation, which
    shrinks as \code{G} grows, and nothing else.}
}
A function that records more about its run adds elements to the list,
and its help page names them. The line "Replications" beneath the
printed table gives \code{G}, and the three counts when a replication
was without an interval. With many replications without an interval the
rows describe only the studies in which the interval could be formed,
so \code{failed} is to be reported beside them.
